WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.709
Require evidence, then verify it in code.

2
00:00:02.989 --> 00:00:06.893
The single most useful check for extraction is also the simplest.

3
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:12.080
Add an evidence field to each item: a verbatim quote from the source that supports it.

4
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.011
Then check, in deterministic code, that the quote actually appears in the source.

5
00:00:17.291 --> 00:00:28.704
Anthropic recommends both halves of this in its guidance on reducing hallucinations: ground answers in direct quotes, and have each claim backed by a supporting quote or retracted.

6
00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:36.771
Asking for the quote helps the model (it has to locate support before committing), but the verification is what gives you a guarantee.

7
00:00:37.051 --> 00:00:40.507
A model can invent a quote as fluently as it invents a date.

8
00:00:40.787 --> 00:00:46.291
Your substring check can’t be fooled that way: either the words are in the transcript or they aren’t.

9
00:00:46.571 --> 00:00:48.235
Make the check strict and simple.

10
00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:57.859
Normalize whitespace on both sides (collapse runs of spaces, tabs, and newlines into one space), because transcripts wrap lines unpredictably.

11
00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:00.315
Then require an exact substring match.

12
00:01:00.595 --> 00:01:03.240
Resist the urge to use fuzzy matching at first.

13
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:13.419
Every relaxation (ignoring case, punctuation, or small word changes) lets more paraphrases through, and a paraphrase is precisely where meaning drifts.

14
00:01:13.699 --> 00:01:22.893
If you later find real quotes failing for trivial reasons, such as curly versus straight apostrophes, add that one normalization and measure the effect.

15
00:01:23.173 --> 00:01:24.389
Know the limit, too.

16
00:01:24.669 --> 00:01:27.101
A verified quote proves the words exist.

17
00:01:27.381 --> 00:01:34.379
It doesn’t prove they support the claim: “Someone should book the venue” is real text, but it isn’t a commitment by anyone.

18
00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:37.731
That gap needs the semantic checks later in this lesson.
